  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2CommonFiltersDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The WinDivert interception filter for the whole preset. It decides which packets enter Zapret2 at all; a narrower filter reduces processing overhead.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2CommonOptionsDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Common startup and Lua initialization options. They appear before the first profile and apply to the entire preset.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2ProfileDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A separate traffic profile with its own filters and ordered Lua actions. Profile order matters when filter scopes overlap.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2ProfileFiltersDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Determine which traffic the profile applies to: IP version, protocols, ports, domains, and address lists.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2ProfileActionsDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Payload and range filters affect following Lua instances until redefined. Lua instances run strictly in their listed order.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2DebugDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enables diagnostic logging to the console, syslog, Android log, or the specified file.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2DryRunDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Checks command-line options and referenced files without starting interception. It does not validate Lua syntax.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2InterceptDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enables or disables interception. With 0, lua-init scripts run and the process exits without intercepting traffic.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2ReassemblyDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Disables multi-packet reassembly for selected payload types; without a value it disables all reassembly.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2BlobDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Loads a file or HEX data into a named Lua blob variable for later use by Lua functions.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaInitDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Executes Lua code or a Lua file once at startup. Multiple lua-init entries run in order.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaGcDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Sets the Lua garbage collector interval in seconds; 0 disables periodic collection.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2NewProfileDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Starts a new profile and can optionally assign its name at the same time.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2SkipProfileDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Marks the current profile as disabled so Zapret2 ignores it during traffic processing.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2ProfileNameDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Sets a readable name for the current profile without changing its filters or actions.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2TemplateDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Saves the current profile as a template that other profiles can import.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2ImportDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Imports a named template: scalar settings are replaced, list settings are appended, and the template number and name are not copied.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2CookieDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Passes an arbitrary desync.cookie value to Lua instances in the current profile.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2FilterL3Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limits the profile to the selected IP version: IPv4 or IPv6.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2TransportFilterDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limits the profile to selected TCP or UDP ports and ranges. A leading ~ means exclusion.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2IcmpFilterDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limits the profile to ICMP types and codes; * allows any type.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2IpProtocolFilterDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limits the profile to raw IP protocol numbers. TCP, UDP, and ICMP use their own separate filter groups.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2FilterL7Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Applies the profile only to flows recognized as the listed application protocols, such as http, tls, or quic.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2IpSetDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Inclusion filter: the profile applies only to IP addresses and subnets from the file or inline list.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2IpSetExcludeDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Exclusion filter: addresses and subnets from the file or inline list are not processed by this profile.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2HostListDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Domain inclusion filter: the profile applies only to names from the TXT file or inline list.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2HostListExcludeDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Domain exclusion filter: listed names are not processed by this profile.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2HostListAutoDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>An automatically populated domain inclusion list. A host is added after a detected sequence of connection failures.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2HostListAutoSettingDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Configures failure detection and the conditions for adding a domain to the automatic host list.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2PayloadDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Passes only selected payload types to following Lua instances. It remains active until the next --payload; the default is all.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2OutRangeDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limits following Lua instances by outgoing conntrack counters: n — packet, d — data packet, s — relative sequence, p — data position; a — always, x — never.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2InRangeDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limits following Lua instances by incoming conntrack counters: n — packet, d — data packet, s — relative sequence, p — data position; a — always, x — never.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaDesyncDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Adds a Lua function call with arguments. Calls run strictly in order and use the active payload/in-range/out-range set before them.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertInterfaceDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restricts WinDivert to a specific network interface and optional subinterface.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertL3Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restricts the common WinDivert interception filter to IPv4 or IPv6 traffic.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertIncomingDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Adds the ports, ICMP types, or IP protocol numbers named by this flag to the incoming WinDivert filter.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertOutgoingDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Adds the ports, ICMP types, or IP protocol numbers named by this flag to the outgoing WinDivert filter.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertTcpEmptyDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Intercepts empty TCP ACK packets on selected ports. This is needed for an accurate n counter but increases processing overhead.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertRawPartDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Adds a partial raw WinDivert filter combined with other parts using OR. The flag may be repeated.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertRawFilterDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Adds one partial raw filter combined with the constructed filter using AND.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertFilterLanDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Controls whether non-global LAN addresses are excluded from interception; they are excluded by default.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertRawDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Sets a complete raw WinDivert filter and replaces the filter constructed from all other --wf flags.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertDuplicateDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Controls protection against running another winws2 instance with the same WinDivert filter; enabled by default.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2WinDivertSaveDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Saves the final complete WinDivert filter to a file for inspection and debugging.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2SsidFilterDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enables interception only while connected to one of the listed Wi-Fi networks.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2NlmFilterDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enables interception only while connected to one of the listed Windows Network List Manager networks.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2NlmListDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Lists connected Windows Network List Manager networks; all also includes other known networks.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2NoFlagDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No description for this flag was found in the loaded Zapret2 help.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaFakeDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>fake sends a separate decoy packet or packet group and does not suppress the original; fooling is normally needed so the server rejects the decoy.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaMultisplitDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>multisplit cuts the current dissect or reassembly at pos markers and sends real segments in their normal order. After a successful send it drops the original unless nodrop is set.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaMultidisorderDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>multidisorder cuts data like multisplit but sends the segments in reverse order, from last to first. The original is preserved only with nodrop.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaHostFakeSplitDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>hostfakesplit cuts HTTP/TLS around the host name and mixes real parts with decoys; fooling must prevent the server from accepting the decoy parts.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaSendDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>send transmits the current dissect with selected modifications and does not cancel normal delivery. A following drop is needed to suppress the original.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaDropDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>drop prevents normal delivery of a matching packet by issuing VERDICT_DROP.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaCircularDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>circular counts failures and rotates through instances marked strategy=N; final prevents cycling back to the beginning.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaMultidisorderLegacyDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>multidisorder_legacy is the nfqws1-compatible variant: it processes separate replay parts, preserves original segmentation, and reorders segments only within each part.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaFakeSplitDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>fakedsplit cuts data at one position and interleaves real parts with same-sized decoys. The decoys need fooling; nodrop preserves the original.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaFakeDisorderDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>fakeddisorder combines reversed real parts with same-sized decoys. The decoys need fooling; nodrop preserves the original.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaTcpSegmentDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>tcpseg separately sends part of a dissect, reassembly, or blob between two pos markers. It issues no verdict; combine it with drop to replace the original data.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaOobDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>oob shifts the TCP sequence and inserts one urgent OOB byte into the first payload, expecting the OS to discard it while the DPI counts it. This is a zero-phase technique with important compatibility limits.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaPacketModifyDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>pktmod applies selected modifications to the current dissect but does not send anything or issue a verdict by itself.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaWindowSizeDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>wssize temporarily reduces the TCP window and/or scaling factor so the server splits replies. It reduces throughput and is recommended only when alternatives fail.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaSynDataDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>syndata adds a blob payload to TCP SYN, sends the modified SYN instead of the original, and repeats the action for SYN retransmissions.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaSynAckDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>synack sends SYN,ACK before the outgoing SYN to confuse DPI about connection direction. It is incompatible with NAT and does not suppress the original.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaResetDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>rst sends an empty TCP RST or RST,ACK packet and does not suppress normal delivery of the original.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ConfigMakerZapret2LuaUdpLengthDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>udplen changes UDP payload length: shrinking truncates data, while growing appends pattern data. Exceeding MTU may cause IP fragmentation or a send failure.</value>
  </data>
